[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM

ESP-IDF is installed in:

  %%ESPRESSIF_IDF_PATH%%

Nothing is added to your PATH automatically. In each shell in which you
want to build ESP32 projects, run:

  . %%ESPRESSIF_PREFIX%%/esp-idf-env.sh

This sets IDF_PATH and puts %%ESPRESSIF_PREFIX%%/bin and
%%ESPRESSIF_IDF_PATH%%/tools in your PATH. Espressif's own export.sh in
that directory works too, and is required once per shell in addition to
the above (see "One-time setup" below).

idf.py on your PATH is a small wrapper, not the upstream script. It
finds install.sh's virtualenv and runs the real idf.py through it. This
is necessary because idf.py's own shebang is rewritten at package-build
time to point at the system Python, which does not have ESP-IDF's
Python packages.

One-time setup per user:

  %%ESPRESSIF_IDF_PATH%%/install.sh esp32

install.sh compiles several Python packages from source (PyPI has no
FreeBSD wheels), which needs the Rust toolchain installed with this
package and takes a few minutes.

Espressif's constraints file forbids compiling cryptography. The first
install.sh run will therefore stop at cryptography. Remove the
restriction and run it again:

  sed -i '' -e '/^--only-binary cryptography/d' \
      -e 's/^cryptography.*/cryptography>=2.1.4/' \
      ~/.espressif/espidf.constraints.v5.2.txt
  rm -rf ~/.espressif/python_env
  %%ESPRESSIF_IDF_PATH%%/install.sh esp32

This package depends on devel/xtensa-esp-elf for a native ESP32
toolchain. ESP-IDF's tool manager does not recognize its version string
as matching this ESP-IDF release and will otherwise silently prefer a
Linux binary toolchain it downloads itself, run under Linux emulation,
if that one happens to resolve first on PATH. To force the native
package to be used, after sourcing esp-idf-env.sh and export.sh, put it
first on PATH and re-run the tool exporter:

  export PATH="/usr/local/xtensa-esp-elf/bin:${PATH}"
  eval "$(python3 %%ESPRESSIF_IDF_PATH%%/tools/idf_tools.py export --prefer-system)"

This will also print similar warnings for cmake, ninja and any other
tool it finds ahead of its own downloads; that's expected, since
--prefer-system applies to everything on PATH, not just the toolchain.

The Linux openocd-esp32 binary cannot load libudev.so.1 on FreeBSD,
which otherwise makes export.sh abort before it finishes setting up
your shell. If you don't need JTAG debugging, stub it out:

  d=~/.espressif/tools/openocd-esp32/v0.12.0-esp32-20240318/openocd-esp32/bin
  mv $d/openocd $d/openocd.linux
  printf '#!/bin/sh\necho "Open On-Chip Debugger v0.12.0-esp32-20240318 (2024-03-18-15:59)" >&2\n' > $d/openocd
  chmod +x $d/openocd

Once install.sh has finished:

  . %%ESPRESSIF_PREFIX%%/esp-idf-env.sh
  . %%ESPRESSIF_IDF_PATH%%/export.sh
  idf.py set-target esp32
  idf.py build
  idf.py -p /dev/cuaU0 flash monitor

USB serial ports appear as /dev/cuaU* on FreeBSD, not /dev/ttyUSB*, and
are accessible to members of the "dialer" group.

idf.py reports "Git version unavailable" because this package strips
.git to save space; this is expected and does not affect functionality.

EOM
}
]
